faqs:
  - q: "How long does a HubSpot implementation take?"
    a: "On our projects, audit and architecture take one to two weeks, and the build typically takes two to four weeks. Portals with more data, more integrations or several hubs can take up to six weeks. Training and documentation run alongside the final build stage."
  - q: "Do we need a partner, or can we implement HubSpot ourselves?"
    a: "Small teams with a simple sales process can implement HubSpot themselves using a checklist like this one. A partner helps most when you are migrating data, connecting several systems, running more than one pipeline, or rebuilding a portal that grew without a design."
  - q: "What should be documented at handover?"
    a: "The data model (objects, associations and the property catalogue), pipeline stages with exit criteria, lifecycle and lead status definitions, a permission matrix, the automation logic, the integration map, and SOPs for daily work. Your team should be able to run and change the system without the people who built it."
  - q: "Can we implement one hub first and add others later?"
    a: "Yes, as long as the data model is designed for where you are going. Define shared objects, lifecycle stages and ownership rules up front, then add hubs onto that structure instead of bolting each one on separately."
